<Project>

  <!-- Imported at the END of each src/ project (unlike Directory.Build.props, imported at the
       START, before a project's own PropertyGroup runs), so $(OutputType) below already has its
       final value by the time this Condition evaluates; a Directory.Build.props-based version of
       this same condition would silently never match, since OutputType isn't set yet at that
       import point. Scoped to Exe/WinExe outputs, so Base.csproj itself (a Library) is excluded;
       only OneDemoSample.csproj actually runs these targets. If this template instance ever
       grows a second Exe project sharing the same output folder (src/Directory.Build.props'
       BaseOutputPath), both would copy the same repo-root files into that same folder rather than
       one clobbering the other's differently-sourced file.

       Deliberately imperative Copy tasks, not declarative "None Include CopyToOutputDirectory"
       items: an item like that, even declared here rather than directly in Base.csproj, still
       propagates through ProjectReference to whatever references the owning project, and
       OneDemoSample.Tests references OneDemoSample.csproj (for InternalsVisibleTo access),
       so it leaked settings.json/settings.local.json into its own test bin too, corrupting test
       isolation the same way putting the items directly in Base.csproj corrupted every test
       project's isolation (confirmed empirically porting this fix from croicu/desk-tools). A Copy
       task tied explicitly to $(OutDir)/$(PublishDir) only ever touches the owning project's own
       output, never a referencing project's, since there's no item for MSBuild's cross-project
       content-propagation machinery to discover. -->
  <Target Name="CopySettingsToOutput" AfterTargets="Build" Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' OR '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe'">
    <!-- The real, committed default config, always copied. -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.json" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
    <!-- Personal, gitignored dev override (see .gitignore), convenient to have alongside the app
         in a local `dotnet build` output so running straight from out/ picks it up. Never copied to
         $(PublishDir) (see CopySettingsToPublish below): the publish output is what ships (the CD
         workflow zips it), so a developer's own local override must never end up there. Skipped entirely when absent, it's optional, a fresh checkout without one
         shouldn't fail the build. -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.local.json" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.local.json')" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="CopySettingsToPublish" AfterTargets="Publish" Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' OR '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe'">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.json" DestinationFolder="$(PublishDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
